 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "This water is only one foot deep. Your car weighs well over a ton. So you drive through, right?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Wrong. One foot of moving water can float most cars.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Here's why. A car is basically a big sealed box full of air.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "When water rises around it, the car has to push that water out of the way.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And the water pushes back up, with a force equal to the weight of the water it moved.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A foot of water around a car can push up almost as hard as the car weighs.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now the tires barely press on the road, so they can't grip.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And the current shoves the car sideways, toward the deep part of the creek.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Two feet of moving water can carry away even big pickup trucks.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And you can't see if the road under the water is even still there.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Floods eat the dirt under the asphalt, like this road washing out from below.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That's why more than half of flood drownings in the US happen in vehicles.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Weather services even have a slogan for it: turn around, don't drown.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So if water covers the road, the safest move is going back.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "What other everyday danger should we explain next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
